Use of Switch: 8/10. Switch enforces two of the four refusals (scoped addressing and author-only cards with HTTP 403), the kill tests check those behaviours, and the Slack card works; but questions are answered through the gateway rather than Slack, and the offline stand-in shows the core logic can run without Switch.

Technical execution: 8/10. Make targets and PowerShell commands, CI on two operating systems, a reproduction on a second machine with logged Switch message ids, and SIGKILL restart tests give unusually strong evidence; author and reviewer on two machines is still untested.

Creativity and impact: 7/10. Sealing an agent session into a PR so reviewers can check selected quotes is a fresh idea that fits cross-team review; but adoption needs the author to run `flip seal`, it supports Claude Code JSONL only, and the README shows no evidence that real teams want it.

Presentation and documentation: 7/10. The room transcript, sequence diagram, Switch feature table and limits section are clear and honest; but jargon (leaf, K1 to K4, G1 to G8, page rule) is dense, and the material does not show the video.

Shortlist: yes. It is a careful, reproducible build where Switch's permission model does real work for the security story.

Weaknesses that cost the most points:
1. The Switch integration is not fully native to chat. People answer questions through the gateway or `flip chat`, because a message typed in Slack has no user id. A reviewer-room rule also has to admit every person in the room.
2. Adoption friction is high and the problem is narrow. The author must seal before review, and the design depends on cross-team PRs written by agents, with no user or team validation shown.
3. The README reads like a protocol spec. A judge must decode Merkle roots, leaves, gates and kill-test codes before seeing the payoff, and the cross-machine two-party flow is not yet verified.

TOTAL: 7.6
